Others’ words, and pointers at sources — quotation, citation, cross-reference, and links.

<section #ag-a | `<a>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<a>`.

<code #code:ag-a-1>
See <a https://example.com/docs | the documentation>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:ag-a-1>
See <a https://example.com/docs | the documentation>.
</minipage>

*The positional URL form — the target is the first argument, the pipe
content is the link text. The most common authoring path. (Markdown
`[text](url)` is not an enscribe idiom; it renders as literal text.)*

<section #ag-bib-entry | `<bib-entry>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<bib-entry>`.

<section #ag-bibliography | `<bibliography>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<bibliography>`.

<section #ag-blockquote | `<blockquote>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<blockquote>`.

<code #code:ag-blockquote-1>
<quote | A short quotation.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:ag-blockquote-1>
<quote | A short quotation.>
</minipage>

*The &lt;quote&gt; shorthand expands to &lt;blockquote&gt; at eHTML.*

<section #ag-cite | `<cite>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<cite>`.

<code #code:ag-cite-1>
The argument is supported by data <cite goodall2024>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:ag-cite-1>
The argument is supported by data <cite goodall2024>.
</minipage>

*A simple citation. The resolver renders the citation according
to the document-level style. For author-year style, this becomes
"(Goodall 2024)". For numbered style, "[1]". The actual rendered
text is generated by the citation resolver, not written by the author.*

<section #ag-doi | `<doi>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<doi>`.

<code-block #code:ag-doi-1>
<meta>
  <doi | 10.1234/example.2024>
</meta>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:ag-doi-1>
<meta>
  <doi | 10.1234/example.2024>
</meta>
</minipage>

*Bare DOI as the typical authoring form.*

<section #ag-q | `<q>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<q>`.

<code #code:ag-q-1>
She said <q | hello> in passing.
</code>

<minipage #mp:ag-q-1>
She said <q | hello> in passing.
</minipage>

*Browsers automatically render &lt;q&gt; with quotation marks. Authors
do not include quotation marks in the content.*

<section #ag-ref | `<ref>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<ref>`.

<code-block #code:ag-ref-1>
<fig #fig:scatter | A scatter plot of the results.>
See <ref @fig:scatter> for details.
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:ag-ref-1>
<fig #fig:scatter | A scatter plot of the results.>
See <ref @fig:scatter> for details.
</minipage>

*Canonical form: @id prefix (@ references; # would declare an id on the
&lt;ref&gt; itself). A &lt;ref&gt; resolves only against a target in scope, so the
demo declares &lt;fig #fig:scatter&gt; first; the ref-resolution plugin then
replaces the &lt;ref&gt; node with a __ref-marker before hast conversion, and
the handler renders an &lt;a&gt; whose text ("figure 1") is computed from the
target's kind and number. Prefix word is lowercase, from the
DEFAULT_PREFIXES dictionary keyed by id prefix.*

<section #ag-when-cites-fail | When citation resolution fails>

An authored citation never silently disappears: a `<cite>` that cannot resolve renders a
visible `??cite: …??` marker in place — the same marker family as the `??ref: …??` an
unresolved cross-reference renders.

A citation whose key is in no library in scope renders its marker:

<code #code:ag-cite-fail-1>
Prior work <cite adams2019> anticipated this result.
</code>

<minipage #mp:ag-cite-fail-1>
Prior work <cite adams2019> anticipated this result.
</minipage>

*(A preview box resolves citations against this page's own library — the seal reads
citations through (#411) — and `adams2019` is in none of this page's libraries, so the
marker shown is the real unknown-key render. In a document with no `<library>` at all,
every citation renders this same marker.)*

When a `<library>` is in scope but a key is missing from it, found keys resolve and each
missing key renders its own marker:

<code-block #code:ag-cite-fail-2>
<data>
<library bibtex>
@book{mead1972,
  author    = {Mead, Margaret},
  title     = {Blackberry Winter: My Earlier Years},
  publisher = {William Morrow},
  year      = {1972}
}
</library>
</data>

Known <cite mead1972>, unknown <cite bateson1904>.
</code-block>

That source, rendered live on this page against this chapter's own library:

<data>
<library bibtex>
@book{mead1972,
  author    = {Mead, Margaret},
  title     = {Blackberry Winter: My Earlier Years},
  publisher = {William Morrow},
  year      = {1972}
}
</library>
</data>

Known <cite mead1972>, unknown <cite bateson1904>.

*(The found key renders its formatted citation; the missing key renders its marker beside
it — a failure never hides a neighboring success.)*

A third failure looks like the first but has a placement cause: a `<library>` may not
live inside an example box at all — one document, one library (#411; a box's citations
resolve against the *document's* library instead). A boxed library is never loaded, and
the box flags it, counting the box's citations that cannot resolve against it, so the
real cause reads from the page rather than from the keys:

<code-block #code:ag-cite-fail-3>
Prior work <cite adams2019> anticipated this result.

<library bibtex>
@book{adams2019, author={Adams, Riley}, title={Anticipations}, year={2019}}
</library>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:ag-cite-fail-3>
Prior work <cite adams2019> anticipated this result.

<library bibtex>
@book{adams2019, author={Adams, Riley}, title={Anticipations}, year={2019}}
</library>
</minipage>

*(Declare the library in the document's `<data>` block instead; the box's citation then
resolves through the seal. The same not-loaded rule applies to a body-level `<library>`
outside a `<data>` block — see the `<library>` reference's Placement section.)*

<data>
<library bibtex>
@book{goodall2024,   author={Goodall, Jane},    title={Reflections on Primatology}, publisher={Field Press},   year={2024}}
@article{smith2023,  author={Smith, Ann},       title={Structured Documents},       journal={J. Doc. Eng.},    year={2023}}
@book{jones2024,     author={Jones, Bo},        title={The Citation Habit},         publisher={Scholars Row},  year={2024}}
@book{attenborough2020, author={Attenborough, David}, title={A Life on Our Planet}, publisher={Witness Books}, year={2020}}
@book{darwin1859,    author={Darwin, Charles},   title={On the Origin of Species},   publisher={John Murray},   year={1859}}
</library>
</data>
